How the work was divided. I fixed every definition and every theorem statement first, in files that compiled with 'sorry' (statements-as-fixed/, with their sha256), and checked the rule's definitions by having Lean evaluate them against the 1265 least sizes that two integer-programming solvers had found (CheckRule.lean: it prints the number of rows, the number that agree, and the rows of each kind; then the rows that disagree):
(1265, 1265, 637, 74, 554)
[]
Four fresh instances of the model then each filled in the proofs of one part in its own copy, under the rules in statements-as-fixed/RULES-given-to-the-four-instances.md: BuildU.lean (the construction when Phi_q does not divide); BuildB.lean with BuildB1 to BuildB5 (the constructions when it does); Weighted.lean (multisets on the group: Fourier zeros, the mask polynomial, the tree, and that every tree is the tree of a multiset); MinTree.lean and MinGroup.lean (the assembly). Rule.lean is mine and has no theorem in it.

